  },
  "json": {
    "exit": 0,
    "stdout": "{\"name\":\"grit\",\"version\":\"[VERSION]\",\"path\":\".agents/skills/grit/SKILL.md\",\"content\":\"---\\nname: grit\\ndescription: Use the grit CLI for version control in Git repositories — status, diffs, commits, branches, merges, cherry-picks, reverts, fetch/pull/push and tags — with JSON output for scripts and agents. Use when a task involves committing, branching, syncing with a remote or reading history and `grit` is installed.\\n---\\n\\n# grit\\n\\n`grit` is a small, opinionated Git client. It reads and writes ordinary Git repositories, so `git` and `grit` can be used on the same repository. It does not mirror Git's commands or flags: there is usually one obvious way to do each thing, and every command can answer in JSON.\\n\\nThis skill was generated by `grit [VERSION]`. Run `grit <command> --help` for the exact options of the installed version.\\n\\n## Rules\\n\\n- Pass `--json` whenever you will read the result. stdout then holds exactly one JSON object; progress and prompts go to stderr.\\n- Use `--filter '<jq-like expr>'` (with `--json`) to keep only what you need: `grit status --json --filter '{branch, clean}'`.\\n- On failure the exit code is usually 1 and stdout is `{\\\"error\\\": \\\"…\\\"}` (or a structured object with `kind`). Exit code 2 is usage (unknown flag, bad `--hunks` ids, …); with **`--json`**, stdout still has `{\\\"error\\\": \\\"…\\\"}` even for clap usage errors. See the docs site [exit codes](https://grit-scm.com/docs/exit-codes/) page for 128/129 and command-specific checks.\\n- Shared flag rules: **`-n` is always a list limit** (never dry-run); **`--dry-run`** is long-only on mutating commands; commit messages use **`-m`**, **`-F` / `--message-file`**, or a positional message; path operands go after **`--`**. Full tables live on [CLI conventions](https://grit-scm.com/docs/cli-conventions/).\\n- Always give `grit commit` a message (`-m`, a positional argument, or `-F` / `--message-file`, with `-` for stdin). It never opens an editor. Use `--author \\\"Name <email>\\\"` to override the author once; `--no-verify` skips `pre-commit` and `commit-msg`. Use `--dry-run` to preview paths, parents, and author without writing objects or updating refs (`--json` omits `oid` and sets `dry_run: true`).\\n- Don't copy Git's interactive modes (`git add -p`, `git rebase -i`, editors). Use stable **`hunks[].id`** from `grit diff --json` with **`--hunks`** / **`--lines`** (see **Recipes** below). `grit commit -a` / `--all` forces auto-staging the whole tree; `grit reset`, `grit rebase`, and `grit history` cover history edits Git would do with reset / interactive rebase.\\n\\n## New repositories\\n\\n`grit init` creates an empty repository. Default branch is `main`, or `init.defaultBranch` from your Git config when you omit `-b`. Use `-b develop` / `--initial-branch` to override. `--object-format sha256` creates a SHA-256 object database; `--separate-git-dir <path>` keeps `.git` as a gitfile pointing at an external directory. Reinit keeps the existing `HEAD` branch (a new `-b` name is ignored). `--json` includes `branch`, `object_format`, and `git_dir` when separate.\\n\\n## Where am I?\\n\\n```console\\n$ grit status --json\\n```\\n\\nPlain `grit` is the same as `grit status`. Keys: `branch`, `detached`, `head`, `target` (the branch your work is headed for), `ahead`, `commits` (up to ten not on the target; each commit row includes `change_id` and `change_id_stored`), `staged`, `unstaged`, `untracked`, `clean`.\\n\\nThe target branch is the first that exists of: the `target.branch` config value, `origin/master`, `origin/main`, `master`, `main`. Change it with `grit config target.branch origin/develop`.\\n\\n## Reading history and changes\\n\\n| Task | Command |\\n| --- | --- |\\n| Uncommitted changes as a diff | `grit diff` |\\n| Changed paths only | `grit diff --name-only` |\\n| Per-file line counts | `grit diff --stat` or `grit diff --numstat` |\\n| The change one commit made | `grit show <commit>` (patch by default; merge commits use combined diffs unless `--diff-merges=first-parent`) |\\n| Several commits at once | `grit show <A> <B>` (`--json` → `{ \\\"objects\\\": [ … ] }`) |\\n| Blob or tree at a revision | `grit show <rev>:<path>` (same resolution as `grit cat`) |\\n| A commit's message and diffstat only | `grit show [<commit-or-branch-or-tag>] --stat` |\\n| Recent history, ten at a time | `grit log`, then `grit log --cursor <next>` using the `next` value from `--json` (passing a cursor to `--before` is deprecated) |\\n| History with patches or file lists | `grit log -p`, `grit log --stat`, `grit log --name-status`, … (same format flags as `grit diff`) |\\n| Commits on this branch not on the target | `grit shortlog` or `grit ahead` (`-n` / `--max-count`, `--behind`, `--stat`, `--name-only`; add `--target <REF>` to pick the base) |\\n| Who last touched each line | `grit blame [<rev>] [--] <file>`; slice with `-L` (`10,25`, `10,+20`, `/pattern/`, `:funcname`); **`--group`** for smaller `hunks[]` in `--json` on large files |\\n| Skip formatting commits in blame | `grit blame --ignore-rev <OID>` or `grit blame --ignore-revs-file .git-blame-ignore-revs` (set `blame.ignoreRevsFile` in config for a default list) |\\n| Ignore whitespace-only edits in blame | `grit blame -w` / `--ignore-all-space` (same spelling as `grit diff -w`) |\\n| Follow copies across files in blame | `grit blame -C` (repeat `-C` up to three times for deeper copy search, like Git) |\\n| Refs that have a reflog | `grit reflog list` (optional `-n` / `--limit`; `--json` → `{ \\\"refs\\\": [{ \\\"ref\\\": \\\"…\\\" }] }`) |\\n| Reflog entries for one ref | `grit reflog [REF] --limit N` (default `HEAD`; branch short names OK) |\\n| What happened to this branch recently | `grit reflog [REF] -n 20 --json` (`--since` / `--until` / `--grep`; `old_oid`, `new_oid`, `date`, `committer`, optional `action`; indices match Git `ref@{n}`) |\\n| Preview reflog expiry | `grit reflog expire --dry-run --json` (no log files written; `dry_run` and per-ref `refs[].pruned`) |\\n| Drop old reflog entries | `grit reflog expire` (gc rules; `--expire`, `--expire-unreachable`, `--all`) |\\n| Does this ref or object exist? | `grit resolve --verify <SPEC> --quiet` (exit `0` = yes, `1` = no; no output; add `--json` only when you need the oid without `--quiet`) |\\n\\nA branch literally named `list` is not the `list` subcommand — use **`grit reflog refs/heads/list`** to show that branch's entries.\\n\\n`grit diff --json` on **`--staged`** or **`--unstaged`** patch output includes stable **`hunks[].id`** (12 lowercase hex chars) and **`hunks[].index`** (0-based within the file). Ids hash side, path, and line bodies (not line numbers), so they stay valid when earlier hunks are staged. Use **`grit add --hunks <id,...>`** or **`grit add --lines path:start-end`** (new-side **`lines[].new`**, 1-based inclusive); commit-range diffs omit `id` for now.\\n\\n**Partial staging:** `grit diff --unstaged --json -- path | jq '.files[].hunks[] | .id'` → `grit add --hunks id1,id2 -- path`, or pick **`lines[].new`** → `grit add --lines path:10-15 -- path`. Mix `--hunks` and `--lines`. One-shot commit: **`grit commit --hunks id1,id2 -m msg -- path`** or **`grit commit --lines path:10-15 -m msg -- path`**. Shelve selected unstaged hunks without touching the index: **`grit stash push --hunks id1,id2`** (optional **`--dry-run`**). Shelve only some paths (others stay dirty): **`grit stash push -- path1 path2`**. Restore the stashed index on apply/pop: **`grit stash apply --index`**. Inspect a stash including untracked files: **`grit stash show -u`**. Branch from a stash (base commit + apply with index): **`grit stash branch <name> [<n>]`**. Drop every entry: **`grit stash clear --force`** (without `--force`, exits non-zero and reports the count; **`--dry-run`** previews). Use `--dry-run` to preview partial hunks. Exit code **2** for unknown/stale ids or invalid line ranges.\\n\\n**Partial discard / unstage:** same ids or line ranges on **`grit restore --hunks`** / **`grit restore --lines`** (default: worktree; add **`--staged`** to unstage selected hunks). **`--dry-run`** previews without writing.\\n\\nRevisions can be full or short ids, branch or tag names, or expressions like `HEAD~2`.\\n\\nCommit rows in `--json` from `log`, `show`, `revs`, and `status` share `change_id` (reverse-hex) and `change_id_stored`. Human `log` and `show` prefix each commit with `change <8 chars>`; `--oneline` does not.\\n\\n## Committing\\n\\n```console\\n$ grit commit -m \\\"Explain what changed and why\\\"\\n```\\n\\n`grit commit` **auto-stages** the whole worktree by default (`commit.autoStage=true`). Use **`grit commit --staged`** to commit only the index (set `commit.autoStage=false` to make that the default). Use **`grit commit -m msg -- <paths>`** (or `--pathspec-from-file`) for a partial commit that leaves other staged changes alone — like `git commit --only`. `-a` / `--all` forces auto-stage; `--staged` and paths cannot be combined with each other or with `-a`.\\n\\n**Partial hunks:** `grit diff --unstaged --json` → pick `hunks[].id` → **`grit commit --hunks id1,id2 -m msg`** (optional `-- paths`). Same exit code **2** as `grit add --hunks` for unknown or stale ids. **`grit commit --patch-file file`** applies a cached patch then commits. Neither mode auto-stages the rest of the tree.\\n\\n### Staging (`grit add`)\\n\\n| Task | Command |\\n| --- | --- |\\n| Stage everything (modified, deleted, untracked) | `grit add` |\\n| Stage only tracked edits and deletions | `grit add -u` (or `grit add --update`) |\\n| Preview without writing the index | `grit add --dry-run` (combine with `-u` or paths) |\\n| Record intent to add (empty index slot, no blob yet) | `grit add -N -- path` (requires paths; does not alter tracked entries) |\\n| Stage an ignored path | `grit add -f -- ignored.log` |\\n| Stage specific paths | `grit add -- path/to/file` |\\n| Apply a unified diff to the index | `grit add --patch-file file.diff` (or `-` for stdin; like `git apply --cached`) |\\n| Stage selected diff hunks by id | `grit add --hunks <id,...>` (from `grit diff --json`; optional `-- paths`) |\\n| Stage part of a hunk by line range | `grit add --lines path:start-end` (new-side lines from `grit diff --json`; mix with `--hunks`) |\\n\\n`grit add --json` returns `{added, modified, removed, intent_to_add, dry_run, count, staged}` with repository-relative paths. Use `--dry-run` before bulk staging to bound agent work; path lists are sorted and stable. With `--patch-file`, paths come from the patch (same JSON fields as path-based staging).\\n\\n### Removing, renaming, and cleaning paths\\n\\n| Task | Command |\\n| --- | --- |\\n| Stop tracking (and delete from disk by default) | `grit rm -- <paths>` |\\n| Preview `rm` without writing | `grit rm --dry-run -- <paths>` |\\n| Ignore missing pathspecs on `rm` | `grit rm --ignore-unmatch -- <paths>` |\\n| Rename or move tracked paths | `grit mv <source>... <destination>` |\\n| Preview `mv` | `grit mv --dry-run <source>... <destination>` (`--json` → `{moves:[{from,to}], dry_run}`) |\\n| List untracked paths to delete | `grit clean` (dry run by default) |\\n| Delete untracked paths | `grit clean -f` |\\n| Remove only ignored untracked files | `grit clean --only-ignored` (not combinable with `--ignored`) |\\n| Exclude paths from clean | `grit clean -e '<pattern>'` (repeatable) |\\n| Clean files but not directories | `grit clean --files-only` |\\n\\n`grit commit` fails with \\\"nothing to commit\\\" on a clean tree unless you pass `--allow-empty`. On detached HEAD it advances `HEAD` without moving a branch; use `grit switch -c <name>` to keep the commits on a new branch.\\n\\nEach commit gets a stable **`change-id`** header (on by default) that survives `--amend`. Disable with `grit config grit.changeIds false` when you need Git-identical commit objects. See the docs site section on change ids for derivation and testing config.\\n\\n## Branches\\n\\n| Task | Command |\\n| --- | --- |\\n| List branches | `grit branch` |\\n| List with filters / JSON detail | `grit branch --list 'feat*' -a --merged main --sort date -n 20 --json` |\\n| Current branch name only | `grit branch --show-current` or `grit resolve --abbrev-ref HEAD` |\\n| Upstream tracking branch name | `grit resolve --abbrev-ref '@{u}'` (full: `grit resolve --symbolic-full-name '@{u}'`) |\\n| Create a branch at the current commit (no switch) | `grit branch <name>` |\\n| Create at a specific commit | `grit branch <name> <start>` |\\n| Create with upstream tracking | `grit branch -t <name> <remote>/<branch>` or rely on `branch.autoSetupMerge` |\\n| Rename a branch | `grit branch -m [<old>] <new>` or `grit branch -M <new>` (force) |\\n| Set / clear upstream | `grit branch -u <upstream> [<name>]`, `grit branch --unset-upstream [<name>]` |\\n| Preview any branch mutation | add `--dry-run --json` (no ref, config, or reflog changes) |\\n| Create and switch | `grit switch -c <name>` |\\n| Switch | `grit switch <name>` (also `grit checkout`, `grit co`) |\\n| Delete a merged branch | `grit branch -d <name>` |\\n| Delete regardless of merge | `grit branch --force-delete <name>` (short `-D`) |\\n| Predict merge conflicts (no repo changes) | `grit merge --check <branch>` (exit 0 = clean/ff/up to date, 1 = would conflict; `--json` → `result: clean\\\\|conflicts\\\\|…`) |\\n\\n`grit branch --json` and `--markdown` list with `full_ref`, `oid`, `subject`, `date`, optional `upstream` (`ref`, `remote`, `gone`, `ahead`, `behind`), and `worktree` when checked out elsewhere. Human list output adds an upstream marker when configured.\\n\\n`grit switch`, `grit merge`, `grit pick`, `grit revert` and `grit pull` refuse to run when checkout would overwrite local edits or untracked files. Preflight with `grit switch <branch> --dry-run --json` (checks only; reports `changed_paths`) before switching. On refusal, `--json` emits `kind: would_overwrite` with `reason` (`local_changes` or `untracked`) and sorted `paths`.\\n\\n## Combining work\\n\\n- `grit merge <branch>` fast-forwards when it can, otherwise records a merge commit. `<branch>` can be local or remote-tracking (`origin/main`). Use `grit merge --check <branch>` to predict conflicts in memory without touching refs, the index, or the worktree. Common flags: `--ff-only` (exit 128 when diverged), `--no-ff`, `--squash` (writes `SQUASH_MSG`; finish with `grit commit`), `--no-commit` (Git merge state; finish with `grit commit`), `-X ours|theirs`, `--conflict diff3|zdiff3|merge`, `--allow-unrelated-histories`, `-m` / `-F` / `--message-file`.\\n- `grit pick [<commit>…]` cherry-picks commits onto the current branch (ranges OK). Use `grit pick --check` to predict conflicts without changing refs, the index, or the worktree (exit 0 = clean, 1 = would conflict or empty stop). `-X ours|theirs` auto-resolves replay conflicts; `--conflict diff3|zdiff3|merge` sets marker style when a step stops.\\n- `grit revert [<commit>…]` undoes one or more commits (ranges OK; newest first). Merge commits need `--mainline <n>`. `-m` / `-F` / `--message-file` set the message for a **single** commit only. `grit revert --check` matches pick’s `--check` contract; same `-X` and `--conflict` flags apply.\\n- `grit revert --continue`, `--abort`, and `--skip` resume or cancel a stopped revert sequence (same conflict model as pick).\\n- On a conflict these commands update the index and working tree with conflict markers, exit 1, and leave merge/pick/revert/rebase state on disk. To inspect a side or recreate markers without resolving, use `grit restore --ours`, `--theirs`, or `--merge` on the conflicted paths (index stays unmerged). Fix conflicts (`grit diff`), stage (`grit add`), then finish with `grit commit` for merges or `grit pick --continue` / `grit revert --continue` / `grit rebase --continue`. Use `--abort` on the same command to cancel; `grit rebase --skip` skips the current commit.\\n\\n## History rewrites\\n\\n- `grit history drop`, `move`, `reword`, `fixup`, `squash`, and `absorb` share `--dry-run`, `--update-refs`, `--empty=(drop|keep|abort)` (empty replayed commits), and `--keep-committer` (preserve each replayed commit’s committer line).\\n- `grit history fixup` accepts **`--hunks`** and **`--lines`** like `grit add` to fold selected unstaged edits into a historical commit without staging the whole file; optional `-- paths` narrows the diff. Without those flags, fixup uses the staged index (same as before).\\n\\n## Remotes\\n\\n| Task | Command |\\n| --- | --- |\\n| Copy a repository | `grit clone <url> [<dir>]` (`--depth N`, `-b` / `--branch` for a branch or tag; hidden `--shallow` = `--depth 1`; `--blobs none` or `--blobs 1k` for partial clone) |\\n| Fast clone for CI/agents | `grit clone --depth 1 …` or `grit clone --blobs none …` |\\n| List, add, remove, prune, set-head, or show remotes | `grit remote`, `grit remote add <name> <url> [--fetch]`, `grit remote -v`, `grit remote remove <name>` (`--dry-run`), `grit remote prune <name>` (`--dry-run`), `grit remote set-head <name> (--auto \\\\| <branch> \\\\| --delete)` (`--dry-run`), `grit remote show <name> [--no-query] [-n N]` |\\n| Fix a remote URL | `grit remote set-url <name> <url>` (fetch URL); `grit remote set-url --push <name> <url>` for push only; `--dry-run` to preview |\\n| Rename a remote | `grit remote rename <old> <new>`; `--dry-run` to preview; renames tracking refs and retargets branch upstream config |\\n| Download without changing your branch | `grit fetch [<remote>]` |\\n| Fetch one branch or ref | `grit fetch origin main` (or `grit fetch origin pull/123/head:pr-123` for a PR) |\\n| Shallow fetch controls | `grit fetch --depth N`, `--deepen N`, or `--unshallow` (same semantics as Git; mutually exclusive) |\\n| Fetch and merge the remote copy of this branch | `grit pull` |\\n| Pull only when a fast-forward is possible | `grit pull --ff-only` (exit **0** on success, **1** with `refused_not_ff` when diverged; branch unchanged) |\\n| Fetch and replay local commits on top of upstream | `grit pull --rebase` (or `pull.rebase` in config; rebase conflicts abort atomically, exit **1**) |\\n| Replay local commits onto another base | `grit rebase [<upstream>]` (default `@{upstream}`; `--onto`; `--check` predicts conflicts without side effects; `-X ours\\\\|theirs` matches `git rebase -X` where **ours** is onto/upstream; stop on conflicts with `--continue` / `--abort` / `--skip`, exit **1**) |\\n| Publish this branch to `origin` under the same name | `grit push` |\\n| Push to a different remote branch name | `grit push origin HEAD:refs/heads/<name>` |\\n| Publish rewritten history safely | `grit fetch`, then `grit push --force-with-lease` (or `--force-with-lease=<rev>` for one ref); stale lease → exit **1**, `\\\"rejected\\\": true` |\\n| Publish all local tags | `grit push --tags` |\\n\\nOrdinary pushes must fast-forward the remote. If the remote has commits you don't, grit rejects the push (exit **1**, `\\\"rejected\\\": true`); run `grit pull`, then push again. After `grit history` edits (reword, drop, fixup, squash, split, rebase) or `grit rebase`, fetch and use `--force-with-lease` instead of `--force` so you don't overwrite someone else's work. `--force` overwrites unconditionally when you really need it. A failing `pre-push` hook stops the push before anything is sent (exit **1**, `\\\"error\\\": \\\"hook pre-push failed …\\\"`); `--no-verify` skips it.\\n\\nAfter a successful history rewrite (drop, move, reword, fixup, squash, absorb, or rebase apply), grit stores the ref plan in `.git/grit/history-last`. `grit history undo` restores those refs when tips are unchanged; run it again to redo. Use `--dry-run` to preview ref movements. Undo refuses when a ref moved or local changes would be overwritten.\\n\\nURLs can be `https://`, `ssh://`, `user@host:path`, `git://`, `file://` or a local path.\\n\\n## Recipes\\n\\nCopy these sequences in order. Each step assumes **`--json`** unless noted. Read the **`fields`** named in the HTML comments (CI runs the same blocks in `skill_recipes_run`). Substitute **`$HUNK0`** from the previous step's JSON when shown.\\n\\n### Precise commit (hunks, paths, or index only)\\n\\n```console recipe:precise-commit\\n<!-- step exit=0 fields=files.0.hunks.0.id -->\\n$ grit diff --unstaged --json -- partial.txt\\n<!-- step exit=0 fields=oid -->\\n$ grit commit --hunks $HUNK0 -m \\\"commit one hunk\\\" --json\\n<!-- step exit=0 -->\\n$ grit add -- staged-only.txt\\n<!-- step exit=0 fields=oid -->\\n$ grit commit --staged -m \\\"commit staged index only\\\" --json\\n<!-- step exit=0 -->\\n$ grit add -- partial-other.txt\\n<!-- step exit=0 fields=oid -->\\n$ grit commit -m \\\"commit one path\\\" --json -- partial-other.txt\\n```\\n\\nUse **`grit add -- partial.txt`** (or path-scoped staging) before the diff when other paths are dirty. **`--staged`** commits only what is already in the index; **`-- paths`** commits a subset without auto-staging the rest of the tree.\\n\\n### Work through a merge conflict\\n\\n```console recipe:merge-conflict\\n<!-- step exit=1 fields=result -->\\n$ grit merge --check topic --json\\n<!-- step exit=1 fields=result -->\\n$ grit merge topic --json\\n<!-- step exit=0 -->\\n$ grit restore --ours -- conflict.txt\\n<!-- step exit=0 -->\\n$ grit add -- conflict.txt\\n<!-- step exit=0 fields=oid -->\\n$ grit merge --continue --json\\n```\\n\\nWhen you need to discard the attempt instead, run **`grit merge --abort`** (returns to pre-merge state). Use **`grit diff`** / **`grit status --json`** between merge and restore to see conflicted paths.\\n\\n### Rebase and push with lease\\n\\n```console recipe:rebase-push-lease\\n<!-- step exit=0 -->\\n$ grit fetch origin --json\\n<!-- step exit=0 fields=result -->\\n$ grit rebase --check origin/main --json\\n<!-- step exit=0 fields=result -->\\n$ grit rebase origin/main --json\\n<!-- step exit=1 fields=rejected -->\\n$ grit push --force-with-lease --json\\n<!-- step exit=0 -->\\n$ grit fetch origin --json\\n<!-- step exit=0 fields=result -->\\n$ grit rebase --check origin/main --json\\n<!-- step exit=0 fields=result -->\\n$ grit rebase origin/main --json\\n<!-- step exit=0 fields=rejected -->\\n$ grit push --force-with-lease --json\\n```\\n\\nAfter rewriting history, always **fetch** before **`--force-with-lease`**. Exit **1** with **`\\\"rejected\\\": true`** means the remote moved or the lease is stale — **fetch**, then **`rebase --check`** and **`rebase`** onto the updated upstream (do not push again until local history includes what you just fetched), then retry **`--force-with-lease`**. Use **`--force`** only when you intentionally overwrite remote work.\\n\\n### Fetch a pull request ref\\n\\n```console recipe:fetch-pr\\n<!-- step exit=0 -->\\n$ grit fetch origin pull/1/head:pr-1 --json\\n<!-- step exit=0 fields=branch -->\\n$ grit switch pr-1 --json\\n```\\n\\nGitHub exposes PR heads as **`pull/<n>/head`** on **`origin`**. The refspec creates a local branch **`pr-<n>`** without checking out the default branch first.\\n\\n### Shallow clone then deepen\\n\\n```console recipe:shallow-clone\\n<!-- step exit=0 fields=depth -->\\n$ grit clone --depth 1 $REMOTE_URL $CLONE_DIR --json\\n<!-- step exit=0 fields=shallow -->\\n$ grit fetch --deepen 1 --json\\n<!-- step exit=0 fields=shallow.removed -->\\n$ grit fetch --unshallow --json\\n```\\n\\nRun **`fetch`** steps from inside the clone (`$CLONE_DIR`). **`--depth 1`** on clone is the common agent default; **`--deepen`** adds history incrementally; **`--unshallow`** removes the shallow boundary when you need full history.\\n\\n## Bundles and linked worktrees\\n\\n| Task | Command |\\n| --- | --- |\\n| Offline pack transfer | `grit bundle create`, `verify`, `list`, `unbundle` |\\n| Extra working trees | `grit worktree list`, `add` (`-b`, `-B`, `--detach`, `--lock`, `--no-checkout`, `--track`), `remove`, `move`, `repair`, `lock`, `unlock`, `prune` (`--dry-run`) — list `--json` includes `is_current`, `lock_reason` (null when absent), and `prunable_reason` when prunable; `move` / `repair` support `--dry-run` |\\n\\n## Tags and config\\n\\n- `grit tag` lists tags (default `--sort=version`). Filters: `-l/--list <pattern>` (repeatable), `--sort version|date|name`, `--reverse`, `--contains [REV]`, `--points-at <REV>`, `-n` / `--limit`. List `--json` includes `target_kind` and `annotation` (subject, message, tagger with RFC 3339 `date`) on annotated tags; human output shows the subject beside the name.\\n- `grit tag <name> [<target>]` creates a lightweight tag at `HEAD` or `<target>`. `-m` / `-F` / `--message-file` create an annotated tag (`--file` is a deprecated alias for `--message-file`); `-f` replaces an existing tag; `--dry-run` previews create/delete. `grit tag -d <name>` deletes one. `grit tag --verify <name>…` checks annotated tag signatures and optional `tree-sha256` claims (exit 1 if any fail); `--no-signature` skips signature checks.\\n- `grit config <key>` reads, `grit config <key> <value>` sets, `grit config --unset <key>` removes, `grit config --list` lists. Scope with `--global`, `--local`, or `-f <path>`. Missing keys exit **1**; `--json` returns `\\\"value\\\": null` with scope/file/line when set.\\n\\n## Staying non-interactive\\n\\n- HTTPS GitHub: run `grit auth` once in an interactive session (it uses a browser device flow). After that, fetch, pull and push use the stored token. When stdin isn't a terminal, grit won't prompt to sign in; it fails with a hint instead.\\n- SSH: set `GIT_SSH_COMMAND='ssh -o BatchMode=yes'` so a missing key or unknown host fails fast instead of waiting for input.\\n- Don't run `grit update` in automation; it reinstalls the binary.\\n- `grit manager`, `grit upload-pack` and `grit receive-pack` are plumbing that speaks Git protocols on stdin/stdout. Don't call them directly.\\n\\n## What grit doesn't do\\n\\ngrit has no TTY or editor workflows: no **`add -p`**, **`restore -p`**, **`rebase -i`**, or **`commit` without `-m`/`-F`**. Partial staging and discard use **`--hunks`**, **`--lines`**, and **`--patch-file`** on `add`, `restore`, `commit`, and `stash push` instead. **`grit tag -m`** creates annotated tags; OpenPGP signing and verification beyond **`grit tag --verify`** are not goals — use `git` when you need full signing workflows. During a merge conflict, use `grit restore --ours|--theirs|--merge` to compare sides or restore markers, then `grit merge --continue` (or `grit commit` for Git-compatible finish). Use `grit commit --amend` (optional message, JSON `amended` field) to rewrite the tip commit. For `grit stash pop` or `grit stash apply` with worktree merge conflicts, resolve paths (for example with `grit restore --ours|--theirs|--merge`) and keep or drop the stash entry as needed. Index-only conflicts with `--index` fail without changing the worktree or index. When a task needs something grit deliberately skips, use `git` on the same repository and return to `grit` afterwards.\\n\"}\n",
    "stderr": ""
  },
  "markdown": {
    "exit": 0,
    "stdout": "---\nname: grit\ndescription: Use the grit CLI for version control in Git repositories — status, diffs, commits, branches, merges, cherry-picks, reverts, fetch/pull/push and tags — with JSON output for scripts and agents. Use when a task involves committing, branching, syncing with a remote or reading history and `grit` is installed.\n---\n\n# grit\n\n`grit` is a small, opinionated Git client. It reads and writes ordinary Git repositories, so `git` and `grit` can be used on the same repository. It does not mirror Git's commands or flags: there is usually one obvious way to do each thing, and every command can answer in JSON.\n\nThis skill was generated by `grit [VERSION]`. Run `grit <command> --help` for the exact options of the installed version.\n\n## Rules\n\n- Pass `--json` whenever you will read the result. stdout then holds exactly one JSON object; progress and prompts go to stderr.\n- Use `--filter '<jq-like expr>'` (with `--json`) to keep only what you need: `grit status --json --filter '{branch, clean}'`.\n- On failure the exit code is usually 1 and stdout is `{\"error\": \"…\"}` (or a structured object with `kind`). Exit code 2 is usage (unknown flag, bad `--hunks` ids, …); with **`--json`**, stdout still has `{\"error\": \"…\"}` even for clap usage errors. See the docs site [exit codes](https://grit-scm.com/docs/exit-codes/) page for 128/129 and command-specific checks.\n- Shared flag rules: **`-n` is always a list limit** (never dry-run); **`--dry-run`** is long-only on mutating commands; commit messages use **`-m`**, **`-F` / `--message-file`**, or a positional message; path operands go after **`--`**. Full tables live on [CLI conventions](https://grit-scm.com/docs/cli-conventions/).\n- Always give `grit commit` a message (`-m`, a positional argument, or `-F` / `--message-file`, with `-` for stdin). It never opens an editor. Use `--author \"Name <email>\"` to override the author once; `--no-verify` skips `pre-commit` and `commit-msg`. Use `--dry-run` to preview paths, parents, and author without writing objects or updating refs (`--json` omits `oid` and sets `dry_run: true`).\n- Don't copy Git's interactive modes (`git add -p`, `git rebase -i`, editors). Use stable **`hunks[].id`** from `grit diff --json` with **`--hunks`** / **`--lines`** (see **Recipes** below). `grit commit -a` / `--all` forces auto-staging the whole tree; `grit reset`, `grit rebase`, and `grit history` cover history edits Git would do with reset / interactive rebase.\n\n## New repositories\n\n`grit init` creates an empty repository. Default branch is `main`, or `init.defaultBranch` from your Git config when you omit `-b`. Use `-b develop` / `--initial-branch` to override. `--object-format sha256` creates a SHA-256 object database; `--separate-git-dir <path>` keeps `.git` as a gitfile pointing at an external directory. Reinit keeps the existing `HEAD` branch (a new `-b` name is ignored). `--json` includes `branch`, `object_format`, and `git_dir` when separate.\n\n## Where am I?\n\n```console\n$ grit status --json\n```\n\nPlain `grit` is the same as `grit status`. Keys: `branch`, `detached`, `head`, `target` (the branch your work is headed for), `ahead`, `commits` (up to ten not on the target; each commit row includes `change_id` and `change_id_stored`), `staged`, `unstaged`, `untracked`, `clean`.\n\nThe target branch is the first that exists of: the `target.branch` config value, `origin/master`, `origin/main`, `master`, `main`. Change it with `grit config target.branch origin/develop`.\n\n## Reading history and changes\n\n| Task | Command |\n| --- | --- |\n| Uncommitted changes as a diff | `grit diff` |\n| Changed paths only | `grit diff --name-only` |\n| Per-file line counts | `grit diff --stat` or `grit diff --numstat` |\n| The change one commit made | `grit show <commit>` (patch by default; merge commits use combined diffs unless `--diff-merges=first-parent`) |\n| Several commits at once | `grit show <A> <B>` (`--json` → `{ \"objects\": [ … ] }`) |\n| Blob or tree at a revision | `grit show <rev>:<path>` (same resolution as `grit cat`) |\n| A commit's message and diffstat only | `grit show [<commit-or-branch-or-tag>] --stat` |\n| Recent history, ten at a time | `grit log`, then `grit log --cursor <next>` using the `next` value from `--json` (passing a cursor to `--before` is deprecated) |\n| History with patches or file lists | `grit log -p`, `grit log --stat`, `grit log --name-status`, … (same format flags as `grit diff`) |\n| Commits on this branch not on the target | `grit shortlog` or `grit ahead` (`-n` / `--max-count`, `--behind`, `--stat`, `--name-only`; add `--target <REF>` to pick the base) |\n| Who last touched each line | `grit blame [<rev>] [--] <file>`; slice with `-L` (`10,25`, `10,+20`, `/pattern/`, `:funcname`); **`--group`** for smaller `hunks[]` in `--json` on large files |\n| Skip formatting commits in blame | `grit blame --ignore-rev <OID>` or `grit blame --ignore-revs-file .git-blame-ignore-revs` (set `blame.ignoreRevsFile` in config for a default list) |\n| Ignore whitespace-only edits in blame | `grit blame -w` / `--ignore-all-space` (same spelling as `grit diff -w`) |\n| Follow copies across files in blame | `grit blame -C` (repeat `-C` up to three times for deeper copy search, like Git) |\n| Refs that have a reflog | `grit reflog list` (optional `-n` / `--limit`; `--json` → `{ \"refs\": [{ \"ref\": \"…\" }] }`) |\n| Reflog entries for one ref | `grit reflog [REF] --limit N` (default `HEAD`; branch short names OK) |\n| What happened to this branch recently | `grit reflog [REF] -n 20 --json` (`--since` / `--until` / `--grep`; `old_oid`, `new_oid`, `date`, `committer`, optional `action`; indices match Git `ref@{n}`) |\n| Preview reflog expiry | `grit reflog expire --dry-run --json` (no log files written; `dry_run` and per-ref `refs[].pruned`) |\n| Drop old reflog entries | `grit reflog expire` (gc rules; `--expire`, `--expire-unreachable`, `--all`) |\n| Does this ref or object exist? | `grit resolve --verify <SPEC> --quiet` (exit `0` = yes, `1` = no; no output; add `--json` only when you need the oid without `--quiet`) |\n\nA branch literally named `list` is not the `list` subcommand — use **`grit reflog refs/heads/list`** to show that branch's entries.\n\n`grit diff --json` on **`--staged`** or **`--unstaged`** patch output includes stable **`hunks[].id`** (12 lowercase hex chars) and **`hunks[].index`** (0-based within the file). Ids hash side, path, and line bodies (not line numbers), so they stay valid when earlier hunks are staged. Use **`grit add --hunks <id,...>`** or **`grit add --lines path:start-end`** (new-side **`lines[].new`**, 1-based inclusive); commit-range diffs omit `id` for now.\n\n**Partial staging:** `grit diff --unstaged --json -- path | jq '.files[].hunks[] | .id'` → `grit add --hunks id1,id2 -- path`, or pick **`lines[].new`** → `grit add --lines path:10-15 -- path`. Mix `--hunks` and `--lines`. One-shot commit: **`grit commit --hunks id1,id2 -m msg -- path`** or **`grit commit --lines path:10-15 -m msg -- path`**. Shelve selected unstaged hunks without touching the index: **`grit stash push --hunks id1,id2`** (optional **`--dry-run`**). Shelve only some paths (others stay dirty): **`grit stash push -- path1 path2`**. Restore the stashed index on apply/pop: **`grit stash apply --index`**. Inspect a stash including untracked files: **`grit stash show -u`**. Branch from a stash (base commit + apply with index): **`grit stash branch <name> [<n>]`**. Drop every entry: **`grit stash clear --force`** (without `--force`, exits non-zero and reports the count; **`--dry-run`** previews). Use `--dry-run` to preview partial hunks. Exit code **2** for unknown/stale ids or invalid line ranges.\n\n**Partial discard / unstage:** same ids or line ranges on **`grit restore --hunks`** / **`grit restore --lines`** (default: worktree; add **`--staged`** to unstage selected hunks). **`--dry-run`** previews without writing.\n\nRevisions can be full or short ids, branch or tag names, or expressions like `HEAD~2`.\n\nCommit rows in `--json` from `log`, `show`, `revs`, and `status` share `change_id` (reverse-hex) and `change_id_stored`. Human `log` and `show` prefix each commit with `change <8 chars>`; `--oneline` does not.\n\n## Committing\n\n```console\n$ grit commit -m \"Explain what changed and why\"\n```\n\n`grit commit` **auto-stages** the whole worktree by default (`commit.autoStage=true`). Use **`grit commit --staged`** to commit only the index (set `commit.autoStage=false` to make that the default). Use **`grit commit -m msg -- <paths>`** (or `--pathspec-from-file`) for a partial commit that leaves other staged changes alone — like `git commit --only`. `-a` / `--all` forces auto-stage; `--staged` and paths cannot be combined with each other or with `-a`.\n\n**Partial hunks:** `grit diff --unstaged --json` → pick `hunks[].id` → **`grit commit --hunks id1,id2 -m msg`** (optional `-- paths`). Same exit code **2** as `grit add --hunks` for unknown or stale ids. **`grit commit --patch-file file`** applies a cached patch then commits. Neither mode auto-stages the rest of the tree.\n\n### Staging (`grit add`)\n\n| Task | Command |\n| --- | --- |\n| Stage everything (modified, deleted, untracked) | `grit add` |\n| Stage only tracked edits and deletions | `grit add -u` (or `grit add --update`) |\n| Preview without writing the index | `grit add --dry-run` (combine with `-u` or paths) |\n| Record intent to add (empty index slot, no blob yet) | `grit add -N -- path` (requires paths; does not alter tracked entries) |\n| Stage an ignored path | `grit add -f -- ignored.log` |\n| Stage specific paths | `grit add -- path/to/file` |\n| Apply a unified diff to the index | `grit add --patch-file file.diff` (or `-` for stdin; like `git apply --cached`) |\n| Stage selected diff hunks by id | `grit add --hunks <id,...>` (from `grit diff --json`; optional `-- paths`) |\n| Stage part of a hunk by line range | `grit add --lines path:start-end` (new-side lines from `grit diff --json`; mix with `--hunks`) |\n\n`grit add --json` returns `{added, modified, removed, intent_to_add, dry_run, count, staged}` with repository-relative paths. Use `--dry-run` before bulk staging to bound agent work; path lists are sorted and stable. With `--patch-file`, paths come from the patch (same JSON fields as path-based staging).\n\n### Removing, renaming, and cleaning paths\n\n| Task | Command |\n| --- | --- |\n| Stop tracking (and delete from disk by default) | `grit rm -- <paths>` |\n| Preview `rm` without writing | `grit rm --dry-run -- <paths>` |\n| Ignore missing pathspecs on `rm` | `grit rm --ignore-unmatch -- <paths>` |\n| Rename or move tracked paths | `grit mv <source>... <destination>` |\n| Preview `mv` | `grit mv --dry-run <source>... <destination>` (`--json` → `{moves:[{from,to}], dry_run}`) |\n| List untracked paths to delete | `grit clean` (dry run by default) |\n| Delete untracked paths | `grit clean -f` |\n| Remove only ignored untracked files | `grit clean --only-ignored` (not combinable with `--ignored`) |\n| Exclude paths from clean | `grit clean -e '<pattern>'` (repeatable) |\n| Clean files but not directories | `grit clean --files-only` |\n\n`grit commit` fails with \"nothing to commit\" on a clean tree unless you pass `--allow-empty`. On detached HEAD it advances `HEAD` without moving a branch; use `grit switch -c <name>` to keep the commits on a new branch.\n\nEach commit gets a stable **`change-id`** header (on by default) that survives `--amend`. Disable with `grit config grit.changeIds false` when you need Git-identical commit objects. See the docs site section on change ids for derivation and testing config.\n\n## Branches\n\n| Task | Command |\n| --- | --- |\n| List branches | `grit branch` |\n| List with filters / JSON detail | `grit branch --list 'feat*' -a --merged main --sort date -n 20 --json` |\n| Current branch name only | `grit branch --show-current` or `grit resolve --abbrev-ref HEAD` |\n| Upstream tracking branch name | `grit resolve --abbrev-ref '@{u}'` (full: `grit resolve --symbolic-full-name '@{u}'`) |\n| Create a branch at the current commit (no switch) | `grit branch <name>` |\n| Create at a specific commit | `grit branch <name> <start>` |\n| Create with upstream tracking | `grit branch -t <name> <remote>/<branch>` or rely on `branch.autoSetupMerge` |\n| Rename a branch | `grit branch -m [<old>] <new>` or `grit branch -M <new>` (force) |\n| Set / clear upstream | `grit branch -u <upstream> [<name>]`, `grit branch --unset-upstream [<name>]` |\n| Preview any branch mutation | add `--dry-run --json` (no ref, config, or reflog changes) |\n| Create and switch | `grit switch -c <name>` |\n| Switch | `grit switch <name>` (also `grit checkout`, `grit co`) |\n| Delete a merged branch | `grit branch -d <name>` |\n| Delete regardless of merge | `grit branch --force-delete <name>` (short `-D`) |\n| Predict merge conflicts (no repo changes) | `grit merge --check <branch>` (exit 0 = clean/ff/up to date, 1 = would conflict; `--json` → `result: clean\\|conflicts\\|…`) |\n\n`grit branch --json` and `--markdown` list with `full_ref`, `oid`, `subject`, `date`, optional `upstream` (`ref`, `remote`, `gone`, `ahead`, `behind`), and `worktree` when checked out elsewhere. Human list output adds an upstream marker when configured.\n\n`grit switch`, `grit merge`, `grit pick`, `grit revert` and `grit pull` refuse to run when checkout would overwrite local edits or untracked files. Preflight with `grit switch <branch> --dry-run --json` (checks only; reports `changed_paths`) before switching. On refusal, `--json` emits `kind: would_overwrite` with `reason` (`local_changes` or `untracked`) and sorted `paths`.\n\n## Combining work\n\n- `grit merge <branch>` fast-forwards when it can, otherwise records a merge commit. `<branch>` can be local or remote-tracking (`origin/main`). Use `grit merge --check <branch>` to predict conflicts in memory without touching refs, the index, or the worktree. Common flags: `--ff-only` (exit 128 when diverged), `--no-ff`, `--squash` (writes `SQUASH_MSG`; finish with `grit commit`), `--no-commit` (Git merge state; finish with `grit commit`), `-X ours|theirs`, `--conflict diff3|zdiff3|merge`, `--allow-unrelated-histories`, `-m` / `-F` / `--message-file`.\n- `grit pick [<commit>…]` cherry-picks commits onto the current branch (ranges OK). Use `grit pick --check` to predict conflicts without changing refs, the index, or the worktree (exit 0 = clean, 1 = would conflict or empty stop). `-X ours|theirs` auto-resolves replay conflicts; `--conflict diff3|zdiff3|merge` sets marker style when a step stops.\n- `grit revert [<commit>…]` undoes one or more commits (ranges OK; newest first). Merge commits need `--mainline <n>`. `-m` / `-F` / `--message-file` set the message for a **single** commit only. `grit revert --check` matches pick’s `--check` contract; same `-X` and `--conflict` flags apply.\n- `grit revert --continue`, `--abort`, and `--skip` resume or cancel a stopped revert sequence (same conflict model as pick).\n- On a conflict these commands update the index and working tree with conflict markers, exit 1, and leave merge/pick/revert/rebase state on disk. To inspect a side or recreate markers without resolving, use `grit restore --ours`, `--theirs`, or `--merge` on the conflicted paths (index stays unmerged). Fix conflicts (`grit diff`), stage (`grit add`), then finish with `grit commit` for merges or `grit pick --continue` / `grit revert --continue` / `grit rebase --continue`. Use `--abort` on the same command to cancel; `grit rebase --skip` skips the current commit.\n\n## History rewrites\n\n- `grit history drop`, `move`, `reword`, `fixup`, `squash`, and `absorb` share `--dry-run`, `--update-refs`, `--empty=(drop|keep|abort)` (empty replayed commits), and `--keep-committer` (preserve each replayed commit’s committer line).\n- `grit history fixup` accepts **`--hunks`** and **`--lines`** like `grit add` to fold selected unstaged edits into a historical commit without staging the whole file; optional `-- paths` narrows the diff. Without those flags, fixup uses the staged index (same as before).\n\n## Remotes\n\n| Task | Command |\n| --- | --- |\n| Copy a repository | `grit clone <url> [<dir>]` (`--depth N`, `-b` / `--branch` for a branch or tag; hidden `--shallow` = `--depth 1`; `--blobs none` or `--blobs 1k` for partial clone) |\n| Fast clone for CI/agents | `grit clone --depth 1 …` or `grit clone --blobs none …` |\n| List, add, remove, prune, set-head, or show remotes | `grit remote`, `grit remote add <name> <url> [--fetch]`, `grit remote -v`, `grit remote remove <name>` (`--dry-run`), `grit remote prune <name>` (`--dry-run`), `grit remote set-head <name> (--auto \\| <branch> \\| --delete)` (`--dry-run`), `grit remote show <name> [--no-query] [-n N]` |\n| Fix a remote URL | `grit remote set-url <name> <url>` (fetch URL); `grit remote set-url --push <name> <url>` for push only; `--dry-run` to preview |\n| Rename a remote | `grit remote rename <old> <new>`; `--dry-run` to preview; renames tracking refs and retargets branch upstream config |\n| Download without changing your branch | `grit fetch [<remote>]` |\n| Fetch one branch or ref | `grit fetch origin main` (or `grit fetch origin pull/123/head:pr-123` for a PR) |\n| Shallow fetch controls | `grit fetch --depth N`, `--deepen N`, or `--unshallow` (same semantics as Git; mutually exclusive) |\n| Fetch and merge the remote copy of this branch | `grit pull` |\n| Pull only when a fast-forward is possible | `grit pull --ff-only` (exit **0** on success, **1** with `refused_not_ff` when diverged; branch unchanged) |\n| Fetch and replay local commits on top of upstream | `grit pull --rebase` (or `pull.rebase` in config; rebase conflicts abort atomically, exit **1**) |\n| Replay local commits onto another base | `grit rebase [<upstream>]` (default `@{upstream}`; `--onto`; `--check` predicts conflicts without side effects; `-X ours\\|theirs` matches `git rebase -X` where **ours** is onto/upstream; stop on conflicts with `--continue` / `--abort` / `--skip`, exit **1**) |\n| Publish this branch to `origin` under the same name | `grit push` |\n| Push to a different remote branch name | `grit push origin HEAD:refs/heads/<name>` |\n| Publish rewritten history safely | `grit fetch`, then `grit push --force-with-lease` (or `--force-with-lease=<rev>` for one ref); stale lease → exit **1**, `\"rejected\": true` |\n| Publish all local tags | `grit push --tags` |\n\nOrdinary pushes must fast-forward the remote. If the remote has commits you don't, grit rejects the push (exit **1**, `\"rejected\": true`); run `grit pull`, then push again. After `grit history` edits (reword, drop, fixup, squash, split, rebase) or `grit rebase`, fetch and use `--force-with-lease` instead of `--force` so you don't overwrite someone else's work. `--force` overwrites unconditionally when you really need it. A failing `pre-push` hook stops the push before anything is sent (exit **1**, `\"error\": \"hook pre-push failed …\"`); `--no-verify` skips it.\n\nAfter a successful history rewrite (drop, move, reword, fixup, squash, absorb, or rebase apply), grit stores the ref plan in `.git/grit/history-last`. `grit history undo` restores those refs when tips are unchanged; run it again to redo. Use `--dry-run` to preview ref movements. Undo refuses when a ref moved or local changes would be overwritten.\n\nURLs can be `https://`, `ssh://`, `user@host:path`, `git://`, `file://` or a local path.\n\n## Recipes\n\nCopy these sequences in order. Each step assumes **`--json`** unless noted. Read the **`fields`** named in the HTML comments (CI runs the same blocks in `skill_recipes_run`). Substitute **`$HUNK0`** from the previous step's JSON when shown.\n\n### Precise commit (hunks, paths, or index only)\n\n```console recipe:precise-commit\n<!-- step exit=0 fields=files.0.hunks.0.id -->\n$ grit diff --unstaged --json -- partial.txt\n<!-- step exit=0 fields=oid -->\n$ grit commit --hunks $HUNK0 -m \"commit one hunk\" --json\n<!-- step exit=0 -->\n$ grit add -- staged-only.txt\n<!-- step exit=0 fields=oid -->\n$ grit commit --staged -m \"commit staged index only\" --json\n<!-- step exit=0 -->\n$ grit add -- partial-other.txt\n<!-- step exit=0 fields=oid -->\n$ grit commit -m \"commit one path\" --json -- partial-other.txt\n```\n\nUse **`grit add -- partial.txt`** (or path-scoped staging) before the diff when other paths are dirty. **`--staged`** commits only what is already in the index; **`-- paths`** commits a subset without auto-staging the rest of the tree.\n\n### Work through a merge conflict\n\n```console recipe:merge-conflict\n<!-- step exit=1 fields=result -->\n$ grit merge --check topic --json\n<!-- step exit=1 fields=result -->\n$ grit merge topic --json\n<!-- step exit=0 -->\n$ grit restore --ours -- conflict.txt\n<!-- step exit=0 -->\n$ grit add -- conflict.txt\n<!-- step exit=0 fields=oid -->\n$ grit merge --continue --json\n```\n\nWhen you need to discard the attempt instead, run **`grit merge --abort`** (returns to pre-merge state). Use **`grit diff`** / **`grit status --json`** between merge and restore to see conflicted paths.\n\n### Rebase and push with lease\n\n```console recipe:rebase-push-lease\n<!-- step exit=0 -->\n$ grit fetch origin --json\n<!-- step exit=0 fields=result -->\n$ grit rebase --check origin/main --json\n<!-- step exit=0 fields=result -->\n$ grit rebase origin/main --json\n<!-- step exit=1 fields=rejected -->\n$ grit push --force-with-lease --json\n<!-- step exit=0 -->\n$ grit fetch origin --json\n<!-- step exit=0 fields=result -->\n$ grit rebase --check origin/main --json\n<!-- step exit=0 fields=result -->\n$ grit rebase origin/main --json\n<!-- step exit=0 fields=rejected -->\n$ grit push --force-with-lease --json\n```\n\nAfter rewriting history, always **fetch** before **`--force-with-lease`**. Exit **1** with **`\"rejected\": true`** means the remote moved or the lease is stale — **fetch**, then **`rebase --check`** and **`rebase`** onto the updated upstream (do not push again until local history includes what you just fetched), then retry **`--force-with-lease`**. Use **`--force`** only when you intentionally overwrite remote work.\n\n### Fetch a pull request ref\n\n```console recipe:fetch-pr\n<!-- step exit=0 -->\n$ grit fetch origin pull/1/head:pr-1 --json\n<!-- step exit=0 fields=branch -->\n$ grit switch pr-1 --json\n```\n\nGitHub exposes PR heads as **`pull/<n>/head`** on **`origin`**. The refspec creates a local branch **`pr-<n>`** without checking out the default branch first.\n\n### Shallow clone then deepen\n\n```console recipe:shallow-clone\n<!-- step exit=0 fields=depth -->\n$ grit clone --depth 1 $REMOTE_URL $CLONE_DIR --json\n<!-- step exit=0 fields=shallow -->\n$ grit fetch --deepen 1 --json\n<!-- step exit=0 fields=shallow.removed -->\n$ grit fetch --unshallow --json\n```\n\nRun **`fetch`** steps from inside the clone (`$CLONE_DIR`). **`--depth 1`** on clone is the common agent default; **`--deepen`** adds history incrementally; **`--unshallow`** removes the shallow boundary when you need full history.\n\n## Bundles and linked worktrees\n\n| Task | Command |\n| --- | --- |\n| Offline pack transfer | `grit bundle create`, `verify`, `list`, `unbundle` |\n| Extra working trees | `grit worktree list`, `add` (`-b`, `-B`, `--detach`, `--lock`, `--no-checkout`, `--track`), `remove`, `move`, `repair`, `lock`, `unlock`, `prune` (`--dry-run`) — list `--json` includes `is_current`, `lock_reason` (null when absent), and `prunable_reason` when prunable; `move` / `repair` support `--dry-run` |\n\n## Tags and config\n\n- `grit tag` lists tags (default `--sort=version`). Filters: `-l/--list <pattern>` (repeatable), `--sort version|date|name`, `--reverse`, `--contains [REV]`, `--points-at <REV>`, `-n` / `--limit`. List `--json` includes `target_kind` and `annotation` (subject, message, tagger with RFC 3339 `date`) on annotated tags; human output shows the subject beside the name.\n- `grit tag <name> [<target>]` creates a lightweight tag at `HEAD` or `<target>`. `-m` / `-F` / `--message-file` create an annotated tag (`--file` is a deprecated alias for `--message-file`); `-f` replaces an existing tag; `--dry-run` previews create/delete. `grit tag -d <name>` deletes one. `grit tag --verify <name>…` checks annotated tag signatures and optional `tree-sha256` claims (exit 1 if any fail); `--no-signature` skips signature checks.\n- `grit config <key>` reads, `grit config <key> <value>` sets, `grit config --unset <key>` removes, `grit config --list` lists. Scope with `--global`, `--local`, or `-f <path>`. Missing keys exit **1**; `--json` returns `\"value\": null` with scope/file/line when set.\n\n## Staying non-interactive\n\n- HTTPS GitHub: run `grit auth` once in an interactive session (it uses a browser device flow). After that, fetch, pull and push use the stored token. When stdin isn't a terminal, grit won't prompt to sign in; it fails with a hint instead.\n- SSH: set `GIT_SSH_COMMAND='ssh -o BatchMode=yes'` so a missing key or unknown host fails fast instead of waiting for input.\n- Don't run `grit update` in automation; it reinstalls the binary.\n- `grit manager`, `grit upload-pack` and `grit receive-pack` are plumbing that speaks Git protocols on stdin/stdout. Don't call them directly.\n\n## What grit doesn't do\n\ngrit has no TTY or editor workflows: no **`add -p`**, **`restore -p`**, **`rebase -i`**, or **`commit` without `-m`/`-F`**. Partial staging and discard use **`--hunks`**, **`--lines`**, and **`--patch-file`** on `add`, `restore`, `commit`, and `stash push` instead. **`grit tag -m`** creates annotated tags; OpenPGP signing and verification beyond **`grit tag --verify`** are not goals — use `git` when you need full signing workflows. During a merge conflict, use `grit restore --ours|--theirs|--merge` to compare sides or restore markers, then `grit merge --continue` (or `grit commit` for Git-compatible finish). Use `grit commit --amend` (optional message, JSON `amended` field) to rewrite the tip commit. For `grit stash pop` or `grit stash apply` with worktree merge conflicts, resolve paths (for example with `grit restore --ours|--theirs|--merge`) and keep or drop the stash entry as needed. Index-only conflicts with `--index` fail without changing the worktree or index. When a task needs something grit deliberately skips, use `git` on the same repository and return to `grit` afterwards.\n",
    "stderr": ""
  }
}
